cicada render <score.cicada> -o <out.wav> [flags]

render a score to WAV

Flags:
  -o <file>                    required output WAV path
  --rate <Hz>                  sample rate (default 48000)
  --bits <16|24|32>            WAV bit depth (default 24)
  --from <bar>                 one-based start bar (default 1)
  --bars <n>                   bars to render; 0 means through song end
  --tail <duration>            render tail (default 3s)
  --dither=<bool>              deterministic TPDF dither for integer PCM (default true)
  --normalize=<bool>           peak normalize to -1 dBFS (default false)
  --block <frames>             offline block size (default 4096)
  --loudness <LUFS>            target integrated loudness
  --true-peak-max <dBTP>       maximum true peak for loudness rendering (default -1)
  --loudness-tolerance <LU>    target tolerance (default 0.5)
  --sampler-baseline           compare one recorded layer/take and 2 ms release
  --max-resident-pcm <size>    cap on decoded sampler-pack PCM held in memory, such as 512MiB (default 1GiB)
  --export <name>              use a named export profile
  --help                       show this help

Use --from 1 for the first bar. The legacy value --from 0 is accepted with a warning and means bar 1. Loudness targets (--loudness, or an export block with loudness) add a static gain. Without a master limiter the direct renderer applies it after the master chain, which keeps crest factor and can miss an ambitious target; the scheduled renderer (arrangements, audio clips, serial track effects, sample packs) applies it before the chain, as it always has. If the score's master chain contains a limiter, the gain goes in before the chain so that limiter holds the true-peak ceiling, capped at +12 dB of input gain and 6 dB of limiter gain reduction; a target that needs more is reported as a shortfall. Sampler packs stay in memory as decoded float32 PCM, and --max-resident-pcm caps their total; a project whose packs decode to more is refused before it renders, with the cap and the size measured in the message. Sizes are whole numbers with KiB, MiB, GiB, KB, MB or GB, such as 512MiB.
